#### Changes in numbers employed
We asked CFMG Executive FGDs to estimate the number of people employed before and after CFM (@fig-et_num_employed). The mean number of people employed per CFMG varied from three in Western Province to 21 in Lusaka Province. The figure also indicates that the numbers of people employed in forest management had substantially increased.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Mean number of people per CFMG employed before and after CFM by province, as reported by CFMG Executives.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-pdf/fig-et_num_employed-1.pdf){#fig-et_num_employed}
:::
:::


#### Full-time senior employment by gender and province
CFMG Executive FGDs also reported on the number of senior males and females in full-time employment in the CFMG (@fig-et_fulltime_gender). Clearly, there are many more senior men in full-time employment than women. According to the response across the 75 CFMGs surveyed, 278 senior men were employed as compared to 56 women.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Number of males and females employed full-time in CFMGs, by province, as reported by executive FGDs.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-pdf/fig-et_fulltime_gender-1.pdf){#fig-et_fulltime_gender}
:::
:::


CFMG Executive FGDs also reported the number of male and female youth in full-time employment in the CFMG (@fig-et_fulltime_youth). Across the sample of 75 CFMGs, 207 male youths were employed compared to 48 female youths.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Number of male and female youth employed full-time in CFMGs, by province, as reported by executive FGDs.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-pdf/fig-et_fulltime_youth-1.pdf){#fig-et_fulltime_youth}
:::
:::



#### Types of formal employment opportunities
Community female, male, and youth FGDs identified the types of formal employment opportunities generated by CFM (@fig-et_employment_types). Clearly, by far the largest type of job created is HFO or Community Scout.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Types of formal employment opportunities reported by female, male, and youth FGDs. Word size is proportional to the number of FGDs reporting that job type.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-pdf/fig-et_employment_types-1.pdf){#fig-et_employment_types}
:::
:::


### Income earning opportunities
#### Carbon finance
We asked the CFMG executive FGDs whether their CFMG had received income from carbon finance (@fig-et_carbon_finance). According to the responses, around one third of CFs have received carbon finance. Later responses suggest this is an under-representation for the number of CFMGs involved in carbon finance and likely reflects only those CFMGs that have actually started to receive payouts from carbon.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![CFMG involvement in carbon finance by province, as reported by CFMG executives.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-pdf/fig-et_carbon_finance-1.pdf){#fig-et_carbon_finance}
:::
:::


FGD groups were also asked whether the community was aware of the carbon credit agreement (@fig-et_carbon_awareness). While most CFMG executive groups stated that the community was aware of the carbon finance agreement, interestingly two stated that they were not, which is a little surprising. Most community female and male groups indicated they were aware of the carbon finance agreement, but more than half of the community youth groups stated they were not aware of the carbon finance agreement. Community youth were significantly less likely to know about the carbon finance agreement than CFMG Executives, but other pairwise comparisons were not significant.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Community awareness of the carbon credit agreement by FGD group.](CFMG-ET_files/figure-pdf/fig-et_carbon_awareness-1.pdf){#fig-et_carbon_awareness}
:::
